From 93c54aa2eb3aafcff54e6454dd1a964cf2e95e5d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 19:09:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08099/11362] The 71 goals that cannot yield a plan, read one
 by one: the prompt never asks

Of 138 frozen induced engines over 21 games, 93 are live and 71 of those fail on
the GOAL, not the dynamics. This reads every one of those predicates instead of
theorising about them.

WHAT THEY ACTUALLY CHECK. 34 of 71 are an unconditional `return False`; 3 more
have no return or raise. So 37 (52%) are predicates NO state can satisfy -- the
bounded search was hopeless before it expanded a node, and that is decidable
from the syntax tree without an environment, a search, or a win example. The
rest are board-wide tropes: 11 "the whole board becomes one colour", 9 "colour X
is gone", 9 a literal HUD row or edge band, 3 object-position, 2 connectivity.
Not one of the 22 PLANNABLE goals is a constant and not one is a uniformity
trope; every plannable goal names a concrete region or object the agent had
watched change.

WAS IT KNOWABLE. Answered from the prompts, not from opinion. Every
object-perception prompt was rebuilt here and gated on sha256 equality with the
frozen run's record (19/20 games, 0 mismatches; tr87's window did not rebuild
inside the bound and is recorded as a coverage gap). Across those 19 plus the 6
best-of-N prompts committed verbatim, the WIN TRANSITION block -- the only
mechanism that shows the model a real self-observed win, added by the 2026-07-29
correction -- appears ZERO times, and `is_level_complete` is mentioned once or
twice in a ~5,000-character prompt, both times as an interface stub. The win
predicate is not being induced badly; it is barely being asked for.

The split: 62 of the 71 are object-perception cells whose window DID straddle a
real level-up (measured: 1 in the window, 0 shown, 1 held out, every game) --
the agent's own rollout had the positive example and the prompt did not. 9 are
best-of-N stall cells where no win existed anywhere. But absence of a win is not
absence of goal evidence: tn36 is a stall game with zero level-ups and five of
its eight candidates still induced a satisfiable, plannable goal by reading the
progress bar out of the deltas they were shown. 28 of 71 sit in a game where
another sample proved the same evidence sufficed.

WIRING. `_split_induce` is not a function -- the name occurs once, in a comment;
it is the fallback branch inside LocalGGUFProposer.induce(). It IS live, and
that is the problem: the live call passes no previous_level_complete_grid, so
`_goal_only_prompt`'s win block is empty and the focused win-condition prompt
contains no grid, no transition, no observation of any kind. Separately, the
WIN TRANSITION block can never fire live at all: next_move appends the level-up
row and _begin_level_goal_episode then sets _episode_transition_start one past
it, so _active_transitions() -- what every live induction prompt is built from
-- structurally excludes the one positive example the agent ever gets. The
plain-path goal satisfiability check is also default-off.

A fourth defect: all 22 engines carrying `_combine_world_model`'s double-import
signature define is_level_complete TWICE, and Python binds the second. The dead
first definition is the one written with the transitions in context; the bound
one came from the evidence-free goal-only call. 12 of the corpus's 13 uniformity
tropes come from those 22 cells.

RECOMMENDED, ONE THING: extend generate()'s existing accept-time defect check
from engine() to is_level_complete() -- it already dry-runs the engine and its
docstring records catching 22 of 36 defective candidates -- and attach the
transitions to the goal-only prompt. Detection escapes the bootstrap problem
completely for the 52% slice; repair does not, and the artifact says so and
states how to falsify it.

CPU only. No LLM, no GPU, no induced code executed anywhere. adversarial_verify
0-flagged.
